## Context

A freelance project to build a hospital ERP prototype on Odoo Community Edition. The requirements covered procurement, inventory, pharmacy operations, billing, receivables, payables and financial reporting.

## Problem

Not every requirement could be supported by the standard Community Edition functionality. For example, the full Accounting functionality is not available in Community Edition.

## My role

Owner: I mapped the requirements into ERP workflows, analyzed the gaps, and developed the custom modules.

## As-is analysis

TODO(content): how the hospital's processes worked before, and the main pain points (IN-05).

## Requirements

- Procurement, inventory and pharmacy operations.
- Billing, receivables and payables.
- Financial reporting.

## Solution design

I treated the platform limits as a problem-solving exercise rather than blockers:

{% Steps %}
{% Step title="Business requirement" /%}

{% Step title="Identify the system gap" /%}

{% Step title="Evaluate native capability" /%}

{% Step title="Design a workaround" /%}

{% Step title="Develop a custom module" /%}

{% Step title="Integrate and test" /%}
{% /Steps %}

For each requirement I asked: what can the existing system already do, what is missing, and what is the simplest practical way to bridge the gap. Where full Accounting was not available, I designed a hybrid approach: standard Odoo Invoicing combined with custom modules for hospital-specific financial requirements.

TODO(content): workflow diagram across Procurement → Inventory → Finance (IN-05).

## Build

I configured standard Odoo functionality where it was sufficient and developed custom Odoo modules in Python for the rest, integrated with Odoo's existing workflows instead of building a separate system. The development environment used PostgreSQL, XML views, and Docker with Docker Compose.

TODO(content): screenshots with dummy data (IN-05).

## Verification

TODO(content): how the prototype was tested (IN-05).

## Outcome

The prototype runs in a Dockerized local development environment, which gives an isolated, reproducible setup for development, testing and iteration before a possible server or cloud deployment.

## Reflection

ERP development is not always about finding the perfect tool. Sometimes it is about working with the tools and constraints you have, understanding the gap, and engineering a practical solution.
